<StoryLayout story={story}>
  <div class="container">
    <section class="section" id="conjecture">
      <h2><span class="num">01 — The Conjecture</span>Twenty-Four Years</h2>
<p>In the summer of 2001, Subhash Khot visited India and conceived a conjecture about the limits of computation. He presented it the following year at the Symposium on the Theory of Computing (STOC) in 2002. The Unique Games Conjecture says, roughly, that for a certain class of optimization problems — systems of constraints where each constraint has exactly one satisfying assignment per label at the other endpoint — no polynomial-time algorithm can reliably distinguish nearly satisfiable instances from nearly unsatisfiable ones. The gap between "almost all constraints can be satisfied" and "almost none can" is, the conjecture claims, computationally impenetrable.</p>

<p>If true, the consequences cascade. Prasad Raghavendra proved in 2008 that if UGC holds, a simple semidefinite program gives the best possible polynomial-time approximation ratio for every constraint-satisfaction problem. The Goemans-Williamson ratio for Max-Cut — approximately 0.878 — becomes the ceiling, not a benchmark. The factor-2 threshold for Vertex Cover becomes a wall, not a waypoint. UGC became a load-bearing assumption across theoretical computer science. A large body of hardness results was stated conditionally on it. No one could prove it. In 2014, Khot received the Nevanlinna Prize for the conjecture and its consequences.</p>

<p>The closest approach came in 2018. Khot, Dor Minzer, and Muli Safra completed the Grassmann expansion theorem, establishing hardness for almost-satisfiable 2-to-1 and 2-to-2 games with imperfect completeness — a major step, but not the full conjecture. The gap between that result and UGC itself held for eight years. Then, in twenty-six days, everything happened at once.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="proof">
      <h2><span class="num">02 — The Proof</span>Family 102</h2>
<p>On October 6, 2026, at 21:58 UTC, OpenAI pushed the initial commit of a GitHub repository called <code>openai/math</code>. It contained 719 manuscripts organized into 372 result families, produced by an unreleased internal model. According to OpenAI's README, the model was posed approximately 4,000 problems, and each result used, on average, three hours of ChatGPT Pro thinking compute.</p>

<p>Family 102 is titled "The Unique Games Conjecture and optimal approximation thresholds." The principal paper, "The Unique Games Theorem," is dated September 23, 2026, and authored by "OpenAI." It claims to prove: for every fixed epsilon and delta between 0 and 1/2, there exists a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from 3SAT to Unique Games over a fixed finite alphabet, with completeness at least 1 minus epsilon and soundness at most delta. If correct, this resolves the conjecture Khot posed twenty-four years earlier. Family 102 also includes independent direct proofs of NP-hardness for approximating Max-Cut beyond the Goemans-Williamson ratio, Vertex Cover below factor 2, Min-UnCut within any constant factor, and directed feedback vertex set — all on unweighted graphs.</p>

<p>A separate result, family 105, claims the full 2-to-1 Games Conjecture with perfect completeness — a stronger statement than the 4-to-1 result proved by human researchers days earlier. The UGC paper explicitly footnotes that family 105 "is not an input to this proof."</p>

<p>The repository includes Lean 4 formalizations for a subset of the results. For family 102, the formalization covers the UGC gap reduction plus four sub-results: optimal Max-Cut hardness, Vertex Cover factor-two hardness, Min-UnCut constant-factor hardness, and directed feedback vertex set hardness. Each has a Comparator statement file — a tool designed for independent checking. The Comparator defines the theorem as a challenge, using Lean's <code>sorry</code> keyword to mark the statement that the proof library must discharge. The <code>sorry</code> does not mean the proof is missing. It means this file is the interface. The actual proof that discharges it lives in the main library, at <code>OAI/Computability/UniqueGames/Theorem.lean</code>. According to OpenAI, roughly 42% of top-line results across the repository now have formal proofs, a figure reached with the October 7 update.</p>

<p>As of October 10, 2026, no independent human has publicly confirmed or refuted the proof. No public report exists of anyone outside OpenAI compiling the proof library and running the Comparator check. These are not minor caveats. The claimed proof has a machine-checked formalization; it does not yet have independent human review.</p>

