    <p class="canvas-note"><strong>The Theorem</strong> — A proof graph assembles node by node on cream. Each new edge fires a violet verification pulse. The finished lattice is dense, alien, and illegible. Three unverified peripheral clusters flare red and dissolve. The structure holds, incomprehensible, then fades to a ghost and restarts.</p>
    <section class="section" id="line">
      <h2><span class="num">03 — The Line</span>What Held and What Broke</h2>
<p>One day after publication, OpenAI withdrew three manuscripts. In "Algebraicity of Weil classes on split abelian eightfolds," a sign error invalidated a stabilization-trace cancellation argument and the construction used by two dependent papers. The three withdrawn papers were:</p>

<ul>
<li>Algebraicity of Weil classes on split abelian eightfolds</li>
<li>Algebraicity of Kuga-Satake Correspondences for K3 Surfaces</li>
<li>The rational Hodge conjecture for products of K3 surfaces</li>
</ul>

<p>Fourteen other manuscripts were revised. Total formalizations reached 300 of 719, approximately 42%.</p>

<!-- Reenactment 2: The Withdrawal -->
<div class="ugp-errata-wrap">
  <div class="ugp-errata-header">history.md — October 7, 2026</div>
  <div class="ugp-errata-body">
    <div class="ugp-errata-label">Withdrawals</div>
    <div class="ugp-errata-desc">In "Algebraicity of Weil classes on split abelian eightfolds" a sign error invalidates a stabilization-trace cancellation argument and the construction used by two dependent papers. As a result, we have withdrawn the following three manuscripts:</div>
    <ul class="ugp-errata-list" role="list">
      <li>Algebraicity of Weil classes on split abelian eightfolds</li>
      <li>Algebraicity of Kuga–Satake Correspondences for K3 Surfaces</li>
      <li>The rational Hodge conjecture for products of K3 surfaces</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="ugp-errata-annotation">None of the three withdrawn papers appear in formalization.yaml. They were never machine-checked.</div>
  <div class="ugp-errata-context">Source: formalization.yaml, verified by search of the full 1,784-line file. The UGC proof (family 102) does appear in formalization.yaml and has a Lean Comparator configuration.</div>
</div>

<p>The pattern is suggestive. The three papers that failed were the ones the machine had never checked. The claimed UGC proof, which has a Lean formalization, has not been withdrawn or revised. But this contrast must be held carefully. Approximately 58% of the repository's top-line results are also unformalized, and only three papers were withdrawn. The formalized UGC proof itself has not been independently reviewed by humans. This is a data point, not a controlled result. It suggests that formalization catches errors the manuscripts alone do not. It does not prove that formalization is sufficient.</p>

<p>While OpenAI's model was producing its manuscripts, human mathematicians were racing the same problem. On September 11, 2026, Dor Minzer at MIT received a text message from a friend asking whether he was close to settling one of the most famous open questions in theoretical computer science. Rumors of an OpenAI proof were circulating. Three days later, on September 14, Yumou Fei, Minzer, and Shuo Wang posted a 95-page paper on the Electronic Colloquium on Computational Complexity (report 2026/179) proving the 4-to-1 Games Conjecture with perfect completeness. This is a weaker result than the full 2-to-1 conjecture — four preimages per constraint rather than two. OpenAI's family 105 claims the full 2-to-1 result. The two are distinct.</p>

<p>Ryan O'Donnell of Carnegie Mellon described the Minzer/Fei/Wang paper: "They solved the problem in the old-fashioned way, with their minds, and wrote it with their own fingers." Minzer acknowledged that his own team's paper still needed fleshing out: "From section 6 onward, there are literally no connecting words," he said of it. "We felt the need to apologize for that." He also said: "There is a lot of value in failing and knowing why you failed. Using AI takes all of this out." Mark Braverman of Princeton, speaking in September before the release, said: "Math by press release is not that healthy for math. There is a lot of uncertainty and unpleasantness that we already are experiencing."</p>

<!-- Reenactment 3: 26 Days -->
<div class="ugp-timeline-wrap">
