</div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="signal">
      <h2><span class="num">04 — Signal</span>What the Proof Does Not Carry</h2>
<p>The Lean proof assistant checks that the proof derives the formal statement. Lean is trusted infrastructure — its small kernel is designed to be verifiable. But "the proof derives the formal statement" is not the same as "the proof resolves the conjecture as mathematicians understand it." Whether the formal statement in <code>theorem11</code> faithfully captures the Unique Games Conjecture as the mathematical community has debated it for twenty-four years is a separate question — what might be called statement fidelity. The formalization scope document matches the paper abstract, but no independent mathematician has publicly confirmed this mapping. Kallel and El Louadi, writing about an earlier OpenAI math release in August 2026 (arXiv 2608.28997), described machine verification as creating "verification abundance while leaving adjudication scarce." Their framework was developed before the October release, but the dynamic they named applies here: the machine can check derivations at scale; it cannot adjudicate whether the derivations answer the right question.</p>

<p>Complexity theorist Dana Moshkovitz has spent her career working on the Unique Games Conjecture. As relayed by her husband, Scott Aaronson, on his blog, she described the proof manuscript as "so horribly written that it's impossible to read it without AI help." The proof, she said, "invents a completely new bizarre code with a noise test" — she called it "some alien craziness." It felt, she said, "like something written by someone who's on psychedelics." Aaronson, a computer science professor at UT Austin with deep expertise in computational complexity, called the day of the release "surely one of the biggest days in mathematical history" — his personal assessment. He also wrote: "it also appears that no human has understood just about any of these proofs yet." This is his observation, not a surveyed finding, but no one has publicly contradicted it. No mathematician has claimed to understand the UGC proof.</p>

<p>The conjecture's originator, Subhash Khot, now at NYU, has not commented publicly as of October 10, 2026.</p>

<p>The response was immediate. The Association for Human Mathematics released a statement on October 7, calling the release "a demonstration of power" rather than scholarship and urging mathematicians to "discontinue their work with OpenAI." Terence Tao reposted the statement on his blog but did not add explicit endorsement. The AHM's organizational standing is opaque: it has no standalone website, the statement was signed by an anonymous "Communications Working Group," and its founding date and membership are unclear. The statement is real; the organization behind it is not well-documented. The AHM also claimed that the Advisory Group on Mathematics and AI had recommended OpenAI stop testing its models on advanced mathematics problems. OpenAI's own framing of the Advisory Group's role was different: according to secondary coverage of OpenAI's announcement, the company stated that the group "will not be responsible for advising us on how to pace our internal progress on mathematics." No primary source for the Advisory Group's specific recommendation has been located. Both claims are on the record. Neither can be independently confirmed from available sources.</p>

<p>What the claimed proof accomplished, if correct, is clear: it would resolve a conjecture that shaped a quarter-century of theoretical computer science. What it left undone is also clear. The Lean formalization answered "does this follow?" — and, by OpenAI's own check, the answer is yes. It did not answer "why?" The three withdrawn papers showed what happens without even that first check. But the checked proof, the one that held, remains a structure a machine can verify and no human can yet read. Subhash Khot spent twenty-four years on a conjecture. If the claimed proof is correct, the conjecture is now a theorem — resolved not by the community that sustained it, but by a model that was never named, in a manuscript that reads, by one expert's account as relayed by Aaronson, like something written by someone on psychedelics. The question it settled is closed. The question it opened — whether a proof that cannot be understood still counts as knowledge — is not.</p>
    </section>
